From: Sergio Marin <sergio.marin@pantacor.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rdawlan: derive the station MAC from the boot card CID

With no mac_addr= on the cmdline the driver still randomises the station MAC
on every boot, which orphans ConnMan's saved services (keyed
wifi_<mac>_<ssid>_<security>), breaks DHCP reservations and defeats MAC ACLs.

Derive one from the boot card's CID instead, via the accessor rda-mmc now
exports. The CID is unique per card, so the result is stable across reboots
and distinct between boards with no provisioning step. The obvious
alternative -- baking a fixed address into the image -- would give every
device built from that image the same MAC, which is worse than randomising.

CID byte 1 is the high byte of the OEM id and bytes 9..12 are the product
serial number, the value the MMC core exports as .../serial; 0x02 marks the
address locally administered and unicast. For the card on the bench
(CID 0353445350333247807978015b017a62, serial 0x7978015b) that yields
02:53:79:78:01:5b.

Order is: mac_addr= if given, else the CID, else the existing
nvram-then-random path. Nothing regresses on boards where neither is
available.

This ties the address to the SD card rather than the board -- moving the card
moves the MAC. Boards that need an address pinned to the hardware should pass
mac_addr=, which takes precedence.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [vendor driver, not upstream]
Signed-off-by: Sergio Marin <sergio.marin@pantacor.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/rdaw80211/rdawlan/wland_bus.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/rdaw80211/rdawlan/wland_bus.c
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include <linux/if_arp.h>
 #include <linux/etherdevice.h>
 #include <linux/hex.h>
+#include <linux/rda-mmc.h>
 #include <linux/wireless.h>
 #include <linux/ieee80211.h>
 #include <linux/kthread.h>
@@ -68,6 +69,39 @@ static char *mac_addr_param;
 module_param_named(mac_addr, mac_addr_param, charp, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(mac_addr, "station MAC address, e.g. 02:11:22:33:44:55");
 
+/*
+ * Derive a stable station MAC from the boot card's CID.
+ *
+ * Used when no mac_addr= was given. The CID is unique per card, so this is
+ * stable across reboots and distinct between boards without any provisioning
+ * step -- unlike baking one address into the image, which would give every
+ * device the same MAC. The chip itself offers nothing usable: it has no MAC
+ * storage, and the SDIO function exposes no serial.
+ *
+ * CID byte 1 is the high byte of the OEM id and bytes 9..12 are the product
+ * serial number, the value the MMC core exports as .../serial. 0x02 marks the
+ * result locally administered and unicast.
+ *
+ * NOTE this ties the address to the SD card rather than the board: moving the
+ * card moves the MAC. Pass mac_addr= to pin one to a board instead.
+ */
+static bool wland_mac_from_boot_cid(u8 *mac)
+{
+	u32 cid[4];
+
+	if (!rda_mmc_get_boot_cid(cid))
+		return false;
+
+	mac[0] = 0x02;
+	mac[1] = (cid[0] >> 16) & 0xff;
+	mac[2] = (cid[2] >> 16) & 0xff;
+	mac[3] = (cid[2] >>  8) & 0xff;
+	mac[4] = (cid[2]      ) & 0xff;
+	mac[5] = (cid[3] >> 24) & 0xff;
+
+	return is_valid_ether_addr(mac);
+}
+
 void wland_txflowcontrol(struct device *dev, bool state)
 {
 	int i = 0;
@@ -128,6 +162,8 @@ int wland_bus_start(struct device *dev)
 	if (mac_addr_param) {
 		WLAND_ERR("using mac_addr=%pM from the kernel cmdline\n",
 			  mac_addr);
+	} else if (wland_mac_from_boot_cid(mac_addr)) {
+		WLAND_ERR("derived mac %pM from the boot card CID\n", mac_addr);
 	} else {
 		ret = wlan_read_mac_from_nvram(mac_addr);
 		if (ret) {
